WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.150
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare. Home to over twenty

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.300
five thousand classes that could teach you
a new life skill.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.070
We rarely witness evolution on a timeframe
short enough for a single human life to take

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:18.650
notice. These changes usually occur over many
lifetimes, the gradual drift of a creatures

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:24.070
DNA to best survive their environment. But
in one case in the 1700s humans witnessed

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:30.539
evolution with their own eyes, and they caused
it. This metamorphosis coincided with human’s

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.560
rapid industrialization. We began burning
coal on levels never before seen, and it’s

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.880
bi-products rapidly changed the landscape
for not just humans, but for the animals that

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.780
shared the planet with them.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.170
The peppered moth was one of those animals,
getting its name from its speckled white and

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.300
black colouring, designed to camouflage the
moth while it lay on lichen covered tree barks.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.250
A black variant was first observed in 1811,
many decades into the industrial revolution.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.579
At first the mutation was rare, but human’s
influence on the environment grew, so did

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:08.960
their numbers. By 1895, 98% of the peppered
moths in Manchester had this black colouring

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.570
[1] Surely this black colouring would leave
them exposed, making them easier to spot for

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:18.460
hungry birds. In reality, these moths had
adapted to be harder to spot in this newly

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.000
industrialised world, one stained by soot.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.869
And it may be time for humans to follow their
lead. To evolve, or die. The rate we have

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:32.770
been spewing these pollutants into our atmosphere
has only risen since this discover. Our carbon

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:38.049
dioxide emissions have risen from one thousand
six hundred million metric tonnes to thirty

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:43.950
six thousand million metric tonnes since 1865
[2] And despite our best efforts, that number

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:50.140
is not declining. Human population and development
are continuing to outpace our efforts to curbed

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.630
our carbon dioxide emissions.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:57.170
Just as alcohol producing yeast will eventually
create an environment too toxic for itself

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:01.520
to survive, humans are pumping the world’s
atmosphere with a gas that will eventually

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.080
render the world unlivable for many, if something
is not done. So we have to ask ourselves now,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.709
are going the way of a mindless single cell
fungi that continue to poison their habitat

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:17.469
until they die, or are we going to recognise
that the survival of the next generation is

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.260
more important?

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.620
Our previous videos have discussed ways to
mitigate climate change, by planting trees

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:29.139
in the Sahara or by using aerosols to block
out the sun. Both are pretty extreme methods,

00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:33.730
and come with some big risks that could lead
to some unforeseen consequences. Instead of

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.280
some risky engineering tactic, what if we
could just suck the CO₂ right out of the

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.739
air, undoing some of the damage that has been
done?

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:47.349
Well, in certain circumstances, this is already
happening. Carbon capture and storage (CCS)

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.730
has been around for years. There are a few
main types of carbon capture, almost all of

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.739
which happens at power plants, capturing the
carbon that comes directly from the plant.

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:03.409
In post-combustion ­carbon capture, the CO₂
is captured after the fossil fuel is burned.

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:09.389
In this method, CO₂ is separated from the
flue gas, which includes CO₂, water vapor,

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:14.329
sulfur dioxides and nitrogen oxides, by bubbling
the gas through an absorber column packed

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:19.450
with liquid solvents, such as ammonia. In
the most widely used system, once the chemicals

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:25.469
in the absorber column become saturated, a
stream of superheated steam at around 120C

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:31.260
is passed through it. This releases the trapped
CO₂, which can then be transported for storage

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.559
elsewhere. [3]

00:03:32.559 --> 00:03:38.229
In pre-combustion carbon capture,­ CO₂
is trapped before it's diluted by other flue

00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:43.870
gases. The fossil fuel is heated in pure oxygen,
resulting in a mix of carbon monoxide and

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.209
hydrogen. [4]The carbon monoxide is reacted
with water to produce carbon dioxide, which

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:54.689
is captured, along with hydrogen. The hydrogen
can be used to produce electricity, and the

00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:56.619
carbon dioxide is stored. [5]

00:03:56.619 --> 00:04:01.609
Pre- and post-combustion carbon capture can
prevent 80 to 90 percent of a power plant's

00:04:01.609 --> 00:04:07.439
carbon emissions from entering the atmosphere.
[6] This is a big deal. The IPCC estimates

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:13.150
that carbon capture and storage has the potential
to make up between 10% and 55% of the total

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.350
carbon mitigation effort until year 2100.
[7]

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:22.919
However, this carbon has to be stored somewhere.
It is most often stored underground in a process

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:29.560
called geological sequestration, which involves
injecting CO₂ into underground rock formations.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.580
It is stored as a supercritical fluid, meaning
it has properties between those of a gas and

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:39.860
a liquid. When carbon dioxide is injected
at depth, it will remain in the supercritical

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:47.000
condition as long as it stays in excess of
31.1°C and at a pressure in excess of 72.9

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.509
atmospheres. Many times, the carbon dioxide
is injected into a reservoir which previously

00:04:51.509 --> 00:04:56.539
trapped oil and gas, since those areas have
natural rock formations that help to contain

00:04:56.539 --> 00:05:01.120
the carbon dioxide. While this might be an
okay solution, no one knows for sure what

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.680
the environmental impact could be if the carbon
dioxide were to leak out into the environment

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.800
in large quantities. [8] In some instances,
leakage of carbon dioxide underground has

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.039
been shown to increase plant mortality, reduce
growth and create potentially severe localised

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:21.270
damage to ecosystems. For this to be a viable,
safe option, the carbon dioxide would need

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:26.139
to remain stored for 100s of years, or even
indefinitely, and the feasibility of this

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:28.110
is not certain. [9]

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:33.490
Other methods of storing carbon include sinking
it deep below the ocean, at depths under 3500

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.389
meters, where it turns into a slushy material
that will sink to the ocean floor under that

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:43.340
amount of pressure. [10]But this method is
largely untested, and again, there are concerns

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:48.909
about what this could mean for marine life,
and uncertainty on whether or not the CO₂

00:05:48.909 --> 00:05:52.379
could eventually make its way back into the
environment. [11]

00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:56.360
There have been more promising experiments
in carbon storage in Iceland, where researchers

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.669
have shown that pumping carbon dioxide into
the volcanic rock underground can speed up

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:05.449
a natural process where the basalts react
with the gas to form carbonate minerals, which

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:11.030
make up limestone. This is an encouraging
development, but has its limitations. It requires

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:16.009
large amounts of water: 25 tonnes for each
tonne of carbon dioxide buried, meaning this

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:20.569
process would have to be limited to coastal
sites. Another is that subterranean microbes

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:25.249
might break down carbonate to methane, another
powerful greenhouse gas. [12]

00:06:25.249 --> 00:06:30.389
And while 80 to 90 percent of a power plant’s
carbon emissions can, in theory, be captured

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:35.409
and stored in one of many ways, what about
all of the other carbon emitting things in

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:41.919
our world? Only 25% of global greenhouse gas
emissions come from electricity and heat production

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:47.749
at power plants. Transportation, general industry,
and agriculture collectively make up around

00:06:47.749 --> 00:06:54.370
60% of greenhouse gas emissions. [13] Is there
a way to capture CO₂ from these sources?

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:59.759
Direct air capture has, up to recently, been
a largely theoretical technique in which CO₂

00:06:59.759 --> 00:07:04.789
is removed directly from the atmosphere. Theoretical,
because doing this on a scale that would even

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:10.499
make a dent has historically been ridiculously
expensive - some experts say as much as $600

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:16.590
per metric ton of carbon dioxide. For reference,
a typical passenger vehicle emits about 4.6

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:21.189
metric tons of carbon dioxide per year. [14]
But recently a team of scientists from Harvard

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:25.780
University and the Bill Gates funded company
Carbon Engineering announced that they have

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:30.699
found a method to cheaply pull carbon-dioxide
pollution out of the atmosphere - they say

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:37.730
for as little as $94, and for no more than
$232 per metric ton of CO₂. This means that

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:42.939
it would cost between $1 and $2.50 to remove
the carbon dioxide released by burning a gallon

00:07:42.939 --> 00:07:48.349
of gasoline in a modern car. And not only
do they suck the CO₂ out of the air with

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:54.000
the ability to store it - they will also transform
the carbon back in to gasoline or jet fuel,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.719
creating net-neutral carbon based fuels. [15]

00:07:56.719 --> 00:08:01.289
While this sounds too good to be true, the
methods they use to pull CO₂ out of the

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:05.360
air is not too different from what has already
been done for decades.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.969
This type of direct air capture starts with
an air contractor, where air is sucked in

00:08:08.969 --> 00:08:13.439
at high volumes. This structure “wet scrubs”
the air by using a strong hydroxide solution

00:08:13.439 --> 00:08:18.729
to capture CO₂ and convert it into carbonate.
The hydroxide solution reacts with carbon

00:08:18.729 --> 00:08:23.879
dioxide to form carbonate ions(CO32−.) This
occurs within a structure which is much the

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:26.370
same as an industrial cooling tower.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.690
The next step involves a “pellet reactor”
where the carbonate ion reacts with calcium(Ca2+)

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:34.300
to form calcium carbonate, in the form of
dried pellets.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:40.039
Then, a circulating fluid heats the calcium
carbonate pellets to decomposition temperature,

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:44.550
breaking them apart to release the carbon
dioxide as a gas and leave behind calcium

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:45.810
oxide (CaO) [16]

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:50.330
Finally, the carbon dioxide is combined with
hydrogen and converted into liquid fuels,

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:55.000
including gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel,
using the Fischer-Tropsch process. This is

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:00.850
a process where a mixture of carbon monoxide
and hydrogen are converted into liquid hydrocarbons.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:05.149
These reactions occur in the presence of metal
catalysts and typically at temperatures of

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:07.930
150–300 °C. [17]

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:12.460
This means the company can produce carbon-neutral
hydrocarbons, meaning if you were to burn

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:17.790
this fuel in your car, you would release carbon-dioxide
pollution out of your exhaust and into the

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:22.431
atmosphere. But because this carbon dioxide
came from the air in the first place, these

00:09:22.431 --> 00:09:27.540
emissions would not introduce any new carbon
dioxide to the atmosphere, and no oil would

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.100
need to be extracted from the earth to power
your car. And perhaps most importantly for

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:37.290
the economic viability of this idea, they
can sell the product, which helps to offset

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.779
costs, allowing them to capture even more
carbon dioxide, to either convert back into

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:44.850
hydrocarbons or ultimately store.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:51.449
And backing up their cost estimates of between
$94 and $232 per metric ton of carbon dioxide

00:09:51.449 --> 00:09:55.390
is the fact that they’ve actually tested
the technology in a prototype plant for a

00:09:55.390 --> 00:10:00.070
few years in Squamish, British Columbia, which
offers a proof of concept that’s way stronger

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:04.650
than simple calculations or computational
models. It currently captures and processes

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:07.940
around 1 ton of carbon dioxide per day. [18]

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:12.560
However, for this idea to work on a large
scale, the process has to be cost-effective

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.300
to implement cheaply around the world, without
the massive costs of constructing all-new

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:22.440
factory parts. In the pilot plant, they pulled
all this off by designing a factory based

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:27.150
entirely on parts that suppliers could already
make cheaply and by keeping careful track

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:31.670
of their emissions and costs at each stage
of the design and production process. They

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:36.730
are currently seeking funding for an industrial-scale
version of the plant, that will use low-cost

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:42.180
renewable energy, that will produce 200 barrels
of synthetic fuel a day, which they hope to

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.589
complete by 2021. [19]

00:10:44.589 --> 00:10:49.660
But how much carbon can they realistically
hope to suck out of the air? In 2017, the

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:55.940
world emitted about 32.5 gigatons of carbon
dioxide. If this technology were built at

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:02.600
a scale to suck all that back out of the atmosphere
at $93 to $232 per ton, simple math shows

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.690
that the total cost would be between about
$3 trillion and $7.5 trillion. [20] That seems

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:12.310
like a lot, but many industries are worth
more than that, including Apple or the airline

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:16.490
industry. Definitely a tall order, but not
impossible.

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:20.519
For this idea to work globally in pulling
substantial amounts of carbon dioxide from

00:11:20.519 --> 00:11:24.589
the Earth’s air, there would need to be
hundreds or thousands of scaled-up plants

00:11:24.589 --> 00:11:29.339
producing hundreds of thousands of barrels
of carbon-neutral fuel to drive down costs

00:11:29.339 --> 00:11:33.920
further, in the same way that solar and wind
energy costs have plummeted over the past

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.050
decades with increasing scales

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:41.190
However, to keep global warming to less than
2 degrees C, the international target to avoid

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:46.721
the most dangerous impacts, we will need negative
emissions, not carbon neutral emissions. We

00:11:46.721 --> 00:11:51.220
need carbon to be taken out of the atmosphere
and stored permanently, or the problem will

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:56.399
only plateau indefinitely. And if Carbon Engineering
is making fuel from their captured carbon,

00:11:56.399 --> 00:11:59.089
this is only a carbon-neutral plan.

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:03.660
But the reality of the situation is that when
you are only capturing and storing carbon,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:07.971
there is no market for that. The only way
to pay for carbon being captured from the

00:12:07.971 --> 00:12:12.600
air and stored, on a large scale, would be
government subsidies, and to rely on only

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.949
our governments to solve this problem is certainly
a mistake. And at $100 per ton at the moment,

00:12:17.949 --> 00:12:22.240
there aren’t enough carbon dioxide buyers
in the market for any other uses to make a

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.240
dent.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:28.279
Thus, introducing the idea of selling back
the carbon as fuel is a way to fund such an

00:12:28.279 --> 00:12:32.839
effort. With market demand and money coming
in, companies like Carbon Engineering can

00:12:32.839 --> 00:12:38.100
improve their technology, expand operations,
store some carbon, and work toward making

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:42.069
sure that less oil is extracted from the ground
over time.

00:12:42.069 --> 00:12:45.459
Critics say that we should simply just not
be taking the carbon out of the ground in

00:12:45.459 --> 00:12:50.430
the first place, focusing on reducing emissions
rather than capture and storage, or capture

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:55.050
and re-use. And some worry that technology
like this will allow us to think that we have

00:12:55.050 --> 00:13:00.430
no responsibility to reduce emissions. And
it is cheaper to not emit a ton of carbon

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:05.980
dioxide in the first place than to capture
it. While these are all definitely valid points,

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:11.319
technology like this can and should play a
role in how we tackle climate change. It’s

00:13:11.319 --> 00:13:15.509
unrealistic to think that every industry,
every consumer, and every government in the

00:13:15.509 --> 00:13:19.981
world will change their behavior in time to
tackle the rising global temperatures, as

00:13:19.981 --> 00:13:25.470
much as we wish they would. And technology
like this will go a long way to help mitigate

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:30.250
the negative effects of industries where a
carbon zero result is next to impossible,

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:34.220
like steel or cement manufacturing, or long-distance
air travel.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:38.620
So this may not be a silver bullet curing
the world of climate change, but it is definitely

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:44.170
a technology to be invested in as a tool in
the toolbox to help solve the problem. And

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:50.279
with direct air capture able to operate anywhere
where there is air, water, and electricity,

00:13:50.279 --> 00:13:55.589
every country could in theory, have their
own supply of carbon neutral fuel.

00:13:55.589 --> 00:13:59.759
In the end, we are not mindless animals who
cannot recognise the effect our behaviour

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:04.180
is having on the environment. There are thousands
of people working to solve these problems

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:09.379
associated with an ever growing human population,
with hundreds of start-ups using technology

00:14:09.379 --> 00:14:14.250
for the betterment of humankind. My audience
is full of incredibly intelligent people who

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:19.120
are more than capable of contributing to fixing
our problems. So, if you think you have what

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:23.360
it takes to improve the world, you have probably
thought about starting a company. You may

